Intravenous (IV) Therapy (Iron, Fluids, Antibiotics)
General Practice Other
Indication requiring rapid systemic administration, Peripheral IV cannulation, Confirmation of patency, Observation for infusion reactions

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-023
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe dehydration and intractable vomiting in a frail elderly patient unable to tolerate oral intake, requiring urgent parenteral rehydration.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an 85-year-old patient with severe viral gastroenteritis experiencing intractable vomiting for 24 hours, completely unable to retain oral fluids, leading to acute orthostatic hypotension, confusion, and profound weakness. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to assess for acute kidney injury, perform peripheral IV cannulation, and commence immediate rapid systemic administration of intravenous fluids to stabilize hemodynamics. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of hypovolemic shock, catastrophic fall, or acute renal failure overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I can't keep a single sip of water down, the room is spinning when I sit up, and I haven't peed all day."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a documented clinical indication requiring rapid systemic administration of a therapeutic agent (normal saline) due to the complete intolerance, failure, or clinical unsuitability of the oral route secondary to intractable vomiting. The symptoms began 24 hours ago with frequent watery diarrhea and severe nausea. The patient is profoundly lethargic and reports extreme thirst and dizziness upon sitting. The nursing staff report no urine output in the last 12 hours. The patient denies severe, localized abdominal pain or hematemesis.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe postural hypotension and profound weakness; usually mobilizes with a walker.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid decompensation, renal failure, and delirium in an elderly, frail host.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Frail, appearing severely volume-depleted, dry mucous membranes, delayed capillary refill (>3 seconds), mildly confused.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, generalized mild tenderness, hyperactive bowel sounds, no localized guarding or rebound. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, weak thready radial pulse, profound postural drop in blood pressure. The clinical state necessitates peripheral intravenous cannulation typically in the antecubital fossa or dorsum of the hand, followed by flushing of the cannula with normal saline to confirm patency and absence of extravasation. The plan includes the slow continuous infusion of the prescribed agent (0.9% NaCl) and continuous observation for acute infusion reactions or fluid overload. Expected investigation results include marked correction of the underlying deficit such as resolution of clinical dehydration parameters (normalized BP, urine output) on subsequent follow-up.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin at bedside; staff actively monitoring.
Medication Reconciliation: Antihypertensives (Perindopril) currently withheld by nursing staff correctly.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Intravenous (IV) Therapy (Severe Hypovolemia secondary to Gastroenteritis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of active diarrhea, hyperactive bowel sounds, and a soft, non-distended abdomen without focal severe pain or bilious vomiting.

Sepsis (Non-GI source) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear history of a primary gastrointestinal illness causing the massive fluid losses, and the absence of a high fever or respiratory/urinary symptoms.

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and hypotension entirely explained by the massive volume loss, with no chest pain or shortness of breath.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Physiological Frailty)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient exhibited acute, severe hypovolemic shock characterized by hypotension, tachycardia, oliguria, and altered mentation, entirely driven by uncompensated gastrointestinal fluid losses. Telephone triage accurately identified the failure of the oral route. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to bypass the non-functional GI tract, secure intravenous access, and administer a rapid crystalloid bolus to restore circulating volume and renal perfusion. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have inevitably led to irreversible acute tubular necrosis or ischemic end-organ damage requiring intensive care admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered strict aseptic non-touch technique during catheter insertion, securing a 20G IV cannula in the right cephalic vein. Administered 1000mL 0.9% Normal Saline over 2 hours via gravity set. Administered IV Ondansetron 4mg stat. Measured baseline and post-infusion vital signs (BP improved to 110/65, HR decreased to 90). Mandated the removal of the cannula and application of localized pressure upon completion by nursing staff.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely reintroduce oral fluids, commit to taking exactly one tablespoon of water strictly every 15 minutes once the nausea subsides, rather than drinking a full glass. Set a repeating 15-minute timer on the bedside clock. By artificially limiting the volume and spacing it out, you prevent the stomach from distending and triggering the stretch-receptors that cause vomiting, positively reinforcing successful fluid retention.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nursing staff to maintain strict fluid balance chart.
DME/Safety (Optional): IV giving set and fluids (supplied from clinical bag).
Follow-up: Facility GP review mandatory tomorrow morning to assess renal function and ongoing fluid requirements.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Nursing staff to transfer immediately to the Emergency Department via 000 if the patient's systolic blood pressure drops below 90 again despite the fluid bolus, if they remain completely anuric for another 6 hours, or if they develop severe, localized abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The IV fluids will rapidly reverse the dizziness and confusion by restoring your blood pressure. The nausea medication will allow you to slowly start keeping small amounts of water down tomorrow.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Alert the nurses immediately if you feel sudden shortness of breath or if your chest feels tight while the drip is running.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your body lost too much water from the illness, and because you couldn't drink, your blood pressure dropped dangerously low. The IV drip puts the water straight back into your blood, bypassing your sick stomach entirely.
Actionable Steps: Do not try to stand up unassisted tonight. Once the drip is finished, follow the strict "one tablespoon" rule for drinking water.

